#heading(level: 1, numbering: none)[Acknowledgements]

This survey is based on the lecture courses “Classical groups and Chevalley
groups” (24 lectures, autumn 1989) and “Recent results on classical groups and
Chevalley groups” (11 lectures, spring 1990), delivered at the University of
Crete and the University of Notre Dame respectively, and on talks given in
1989–1991 at universities in Poland, the United States, Japan, Italy, France,
and the United Kingdom. These included Wrocław, Penn State, Northwestern,
Oregon, Caltech, Yale, Virginia, Tsukuba, Kumamoto, Milan, Rome II, Paris VII,
Warwick, Cambridge, and Newcastle upon Tyne. I sincerely thank D. I. Deriziotis,
A. J. Hahn, and O. T. O'Meara for arranging my stays in Greece and the United
States during the academic year 1989/90. The Cretan course contained many
details and proofs omitted here; the notes taken by I. Antoniadis and A.
Fakiolas were a great help.

During the 1980s the theory of classical groups over rings developed to a large
extent in Russia. I benefited from many discussions with Z. I. Borewicz, V. N.
Gerasimov, I. Z. Golubchik, V. I. Kopeiko, A. V. Mikhalev, E. B. Vinberg, A. E.
Zalesskii, E. I. Zelmanov, and others. #source(318) Constant contact with A. A.
Suslin was especially important for the development of my views. I am grateful
to A. V. Stepanov, who allowed me to develop his idea long before it appeared in
his own publications. Much of the work arose from longstanding collaboration
with E. B. Plotkin. Although for many years I could rarely meet L. N.
Vaserstein, we kept in touch. Conversations and correspondence with M.
Aschbacher, B. N. Cooperstein, and I. Frenkel on representations, and with A.
Bak, A. J. Hahn, W. van der Kallen, and M. R. Stein on structure theorems, have
been particularly useful.

Special thanks are due to E. Abe, whose work and deep understanding, continuing
interest, and encouragement profoundly influenced this survey. He arranged my
visit to Japan in August–September 1990. I greatly appreciate the hospitality of
my Japanese colleagues, especially J. Morita and K. Suzuki, and the excellent
organisation of the Hiroshima conference by K. Yamaguti and N. Kawamoto. N.
Kamiya supported me in an unexpected way during the difficult January of 1991.

The final text was prepared at Warwick in spring 1991 under a grant from the
United Kingdom's Science and Engineering Research Council. I appreciate the
friendly, relaxed atmosphere of the Mathematics Department and the help of my
colleagues, especially R. W. Carter. B. Westbury carefully read the final text
and made many passages less ambiguous; he also corrected most of its unusual
English expressions. The remaining ones were retained deliberately. M. Beynon
and J. Rhodes helped resolve problems caused by incompatible computer systems.
E. Shiels was exceptionally considerate and made my life here easier in many
ways.

I was halfway through the text when the news arrived that an overwhelming
majority of my fellow citizens had voted to restore the true name of my home
city. I dedicate this work to the city of Saint Peter, which, despite everything
the spirits of evil have done to it, is the best city in the world.
